<!-- Dedicated baseline for the async insert queue route of INSERT ... SELECT (see
     InterpreterInsertQuery::addInsertToSelectPipeline and AsyncInsertQueueTransform), so a
     regression in that specific route is not masked by every other insert test, which does not
     set async_insert explicitly and mostly exercises the bulk, pass-through shape instead.

     The first query: baseline, measure defaults

     The second query uses wait_for_async_insert = 0: with the wait on, each run blocks until its
     own flush finishes, so consecutive runs can never overlap in the queue and every batch is a
     batch of one, making the measurement dominated by async_insert_busy_timeout_min_ms rather
     than by the route itself. Fire and forget lets consecutive runs share the batching window
     and queue key, so batching is actually exercised.

     This means the query measures only the read plus enqueue path; the write happens in the
     flush and is not attributed here, so the measured time can come out below the synchronous
     comparator, and a regression inside the flush is not visible in this query.

     The third query is the same insert shape with async_insert = 0, so it takes the ordinary
     synchronous route; a change specific to the queue route shows up as a change in the second
     query relative to the second. -->
<test>
    <create_query>CREATE TABLE insert_select_async_insert_queue (n UInt64) ENGINE = MergeTree ORDER BY n</create_query>

    <query>INSERT INTO insert_select_async_insert_queue SELECT number FROM numbers(100)</query>
    <query>INSERT INTO insert_select_async_insert_queue SELECT number FROM numbers(100) SETTINGS async_insert = 1, wait_for_async_insert = 0</query>
    <query>INSERT INTO insert_select_async_insert_queue SELECT number FROM numbers(100) SETTINGS async_insert = 0</query>

    <drop_query>DROP TABLE IF EXISTS insert_select_async_insert_queue</drop_query>
</test>
